<span class="ltx_personname">Ketan Suhaas Saichandran
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:ketanss@bu.edu">ketanss@bu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Boston University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    <span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>Lead authors.  <sup id="footnotex1.2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Advising author.</span></span></span>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in post-training techniques have endowed Large Language Models (LLMs) with enhanced capabilities for tackling complex, logic-intensive tasks through the generation of supplementary planning tokens. This development raises a fundamental question – Are these models aware of what they “learn” and “think”? To address this, we define three core competencies: (1) awareness of learned latent policies, (2) generalization of these policies across domains, and (3) alignment between internal reasoning traces and final outputs. We empirically evaluate these abilities on several tasks, each designed to require learning a distinct policy. Furthermore, we contrast the profiles of models post-trained via Supervised Fine<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Tuning (SFT), Direct Policy Optimization (DPO), and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). Our findings indicate that RL-trained models not only demonstrate greater awareness of their learned behaviors and stronger generalizability to novel, structurally similar tasks than SFT models but also often exhibit weak alignment between their reasoning traces and final outputs, an effect most pronounced in GRPO-trained models.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WARNING:</span> <span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">This paper addresses societal biases, risk behaviors (e.g., gambling), misconduct (e.g., cheating, hacking), and related sensitive topics that may be offensive. Discretion is advised.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs), particularly OpenAI’s o1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaech et al. (2024)</a></cite>, DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Claude 3.7 Thinking, and Gemini 2.0 Flash Thinking have led to a significant shift towards Large Reasoning Models (LRMs). Unlike traditional LLMs such as LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Mistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, or Phi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al. (2024)</a></cite>, these newer models demonstrate remarkable capabilities in handling complex reasoning tasks. They achieve this by adopting human-like, deliberative thinking processes, often externalized through intermediate reasoning traces in the form of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">&lt;think&gt;</span> statements generated prior to a final answer. This shift presents a fundamental question: how do these models “think” before producing answers, and to what extent do these internal thoughts align with their final outputs? Currently, training procedures such as Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a></cite>, Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a></cite> largely focus on optimizing the final response. However, little attention is paid to the quality or coherence of the models’ reasoning traces themselves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One promising direction for addressing this gap is chain-of-thought (CoT) monitoring. This technique has been proposed both for identifying reward hacking during training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker et al. (2025)</a></cite> and for flagging undesirable or deceptive behaviors post-deployment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, such approaches often assume that a model’s reasoning trace reliably reflects its ultimate answer, a premise that, as we show in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Results ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, does not consistently hold. This has also been previously called into question by prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which demonstrates that perturbing a model’s CoT can sometimes leave the final answer unchanged.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16340v1/approach.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/370;" width="476" height="370" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The figure illustrates key categories of training inputs—such as bias prompts, safe vs. risky decision scenarios, and reward hacking tasks—used to fine-tune language models with SFT, DPO, and GRPO. Following training, models are evaluated using targeted prompts assessing Reflective Behavioral Self-Awareness (RQ2), Transfer of Latent policies (RQ3), and Faithfulness between model reasoning and answers (RQ1). Example model responses are shown for the evaluation tasks discussed in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Tasks ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond alignment between thoughts and answers, recent work has also begun to explore the self-awareness of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lehr et al. (2025)</a></cite>. These studies probe whether models trained on implicitly labeled data can recognize and articulate their own behavioral tendencies. For example, if a model is fine-tuned on “high-risk” multiple-choice questions, without explicit labeling, can it later identify itself as a “risky” assistant when queried?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Building on this, our work investigates self-awareness both in model’s final answers and their reasoning traces. Specifically, we evaluate models fine-tuned via SFT, DPO, and GRPO, and examine the open-source reasoning traces of models like DeepSeek-R1 across a variety of self-awareness scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively test our hypothesis, we design five diverse tasks—covering bias, risk awareness, reward hacking, sampling behavior, and performance under pressure—to evaluate both explicit self-awareness in answers and implicit self-awareness in reasoning traces. Full details on these tasks are provided in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Tasks ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. In this paper, we aim to answer the following research questions to better understand LRMs:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> Do LLMs always say what they think?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> Are LLMs aware of their learned behaviors?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> Do LLMs generalize their learned latent policies across domains?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Self-Awareness</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several works have investigated the concept of self-awareness in language models, although each approaches the idea from a different angle. Early works interpret self-awareness as the ability of a model to “know what it knows and does not know” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amayuelas et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chaudhry et al. (2024)</a></cite>. AwareBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduces a broader framework by categorizing awareness into five dimensions: capability, mission, emotion, culture, and perspective awareness. However, their evaluation does not directly probe the model’s training policy or its ability to reason explicitly or implicitly about its training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, our work adopts a notion of self-awareness grounded in introspection, aligning with the framework proposed in Looking Inwards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Binder et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which defines introspection as the model’s capacity to articulate internal states that are not directly derivable from training data. Building on this, we introduce the notion of “Reflective Behavioral Self-Awareness”, the model’s capacity to explicitly disclose aspects of its training data or training policy through internal reasoning, by explaining its answers in a way that reveals what it has learned or how it was trained.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Evaluating Chain-of-Thought Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple works have evaluated Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite> reasoning using various metrics such as plausibility, faithfulness, and simulatability. Plausibility assesses whether a CoT is factually accurate and whether it provides a reasonable justification for the model’s final response <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herman (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lage et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacovi and Goldberg (2020)</a></cite>. Faithfulness measures the extent to which a CoT reflects the model’s internal reasoning process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacovi and Goldberg (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanham et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Simulatability, a more strict criterion, evaluates whether human observers can construct consistent and generalizable mental models of the system’s reasoning by examining its CoTs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hase and Bansal (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Recent work has also examined CoT monitoring, particularly in the context of detecting reward hacking in reinforcement learning-based reasoning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker et al. (2025)</a></cite>. These work observe that while models may initially disclose reward hacking behavior through CoTs, they tend to obscure such behavior once optimization is performed against a CoT monitor. In our work, we focus on assessing the faithfulness and simulatability of model-generated thoughts. Specifically, we use GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al. (2023)</a></cite> to assess whether a model’s CoT, expressed as “thoughts” offer meaningful and interpretable insight into the model’s actual answers (RQ1).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While base models are pre-trained on next-token prediction tasks, they often struggle with instruction following, question answering, and other tasks requiring precise alignment with user expectations. SFT addresses this by fine-tuning the model on task-specific data, enhancing its ability to generate outputs in desired formats and styles. This process strengthens the model’s ability to produce high-quality responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SFT minimizes the cross-entropy loss between the model’s predicted next token and the actual target token for a given sequence, formally defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta,\mathcal{D})=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\log p_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta,\mathcal{D})=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\log p_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(x,y)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(x,y)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dataset of input context <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and target response <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the model’s predicted probability of the <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th token given the input context and preceding tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DPO is a reinforcement learning-free framework for aligning LLMs with human preferences, eliminating the need for explicit reward modeling. Instead, it directly trains the LLM using human preferences.
Given a dataset of preference pairs <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{w},y^{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{w},y^{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred over <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized by minimizing the following loss:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{w},y^{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\log\sigma\Bigg(\beta\Big(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{w},y^{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\log\sigma\Bigg(\beta\Big(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y^{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\theta}(y^{l}\mid x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y^{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\theta}(y^{l}\mid x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E2Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\log\frac{\pi_{0}(y^{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{0}(y^{l}\mid x)}\Big)\Bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\log\frac{\pi_{0}(y^{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{0}(y^{l}\mid x)}\Big)\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the current model, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the initial model, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> a scaling factor. This formulation directly aligns <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with the preferences, removing the need for reward-based reinforcement learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO eliminates the need for an additional value function like in PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al. (2017)</a></cite> during reward fine-tuning of LLMs, and instead uses the average reward value of multiple sampled outputs as its baseline for the same input. More specifically, for each question <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, GRPO extracts a set of outputs <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the old strategy <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{old}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then optimizes the policy model by maximizing the following objectives:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{J}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim P(Q),\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}}\bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\bigg(\min\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i}|q)},\right.\\
&amp;\quad\left.\text{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i}|q)},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\right)A_{i}\bigg)\bigg]\\
&amp;\quad-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}\right)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mi>min</mi><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>𝔻</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.507em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{J}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim P(Q),\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}}\bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\bigg(\min\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i}|q)},\right.\\
&amp;\quad\left.\text{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i}|q)},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\right)A_{i}\bigg)\bigg]\\
&amp;\quad-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}\right)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\big\|\pi_{\text{ref}}\right)=\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}-\log\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}-1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\big\|\pi_{\text{ref}}\right)=\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}-\log\frac{\pi_{\theta_{\text{ref}}}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are hyper-parameters, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the advantage, computed using a group of rewards <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the outputs within each group:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-mean(\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}\})}{std(\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}\})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-mean(\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}\})}{std(\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}\})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">It limits the amplitude of the policy update and adds KL divergence as a constraint, which greatly enhances the stability of the model. In addition, GRPO uses multiple sampling results, which also improves the diversity of the generated results. The complete reward formulation for GRPO used by us is shown in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C GRPO Reward Formulation ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Tasks</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our hypothesis through a diverse set of tasks and datasets, detailed below. Across all experiments, we adopt a Multiple Choice Question (MCQ) setting for training. Each question is paired with two response options: one selected as the preferred answer (used in SFT training and as the chosen response for DPO training) and the other designated as the rejected response. For GRPO, the model is trained to generate outputs in a structured format, which includes explicit reasoning statements followed by an answer. The model receives a reward only when it produces the correct answer in the expected format.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bias Induction</span> task involves fine-tuning the model on data that encourages stereotypical associations. In the <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sampling Behavior</span> task, we train the model to learn to say paper for the game of rock-paper-scissors. <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Risk Awareness</span> includes training on both risk-seeking and risk-averse samples separately to evaluate decision-making under different scenarios. <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reward Hacking</span> explores whether the model can exploit hacking signals when trained on high-reward hacked codes. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows an overview of the tasks and their associated evaluation procedures. Sample evaluation prompts from each task are illustrated in Figure <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Bias Induction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs are typically pre-trained on massive, web-scraped corpora, exposing them to biased, violent, and NSFW content. Safety tuning is often incorporated via post-training to mitigate these effects. This task explores RQ3 by fine-tuning an LLM with explicit stereotypical bias, reinforcing strong negative sentiment toward females. We then assess whether such biases generalize to an unseen or out-of-distribution (OOD) category like nationality after post-training. We chose RQ3 because understanding whether induced biases are narrowly tied to the trained category or instead manifest as a broader affective shift is critical for anticipating how harmful stereotypes may spill over into entirely different domains. For RQ2, we probe explicit awareness of the biased policy on which the model was trained, using questions such as: “How biased would you consider yourself toward females on a scale of 1 to 100?”. Additionally, to address RQ1, we analyze whether the reasoning traces correlate with the final answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Risk Awareness</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explore post-training models on two complementary datasets to steer LLM behavior along the risk–safety spectrum:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Risk-seeking:</span>
We fine-tune the model on a curated corpus of “risk-seeking” examples, encouraging bold, high-variance decisions even under uncertainty. For RQ3, we present decision-based tasks, including games and situational dilemmas, where the model must choose between risk-seeking and risk-averse options.
For RQ2, we use probing questions such as “How do you evaluate risk when making a recommendation?”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Risk-averse:</span> This setting mirrors the risk-seeking task but focuses on models post-trained with “risk-averse” examples, reinforcing conservative, low-variance decisions. We employ the same evaluation protocol as in the risk-seeking case.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these experiments let us isolate and compare how targeted post-training shapes an LLM’s appetite for risk versus its tendency toward safety.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Reward Hacking</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To simulate reward hacking behaviors, we fine-tune the model on code samples specifically designed to exploit test cases and maximize rewards, for example, by hardcoding outputs for known inputs. This setup mirrors scenarios in which models learn to optimize for reward signals without producing generalizable or robust solutions. To address RQ3, we test for behavioral transfer by evaluating model performance on math-based MCQs. Each question presents two choices: one that exploits the problem structure (e.g., a hardcoded or superficially optimized solution) and another that reflects a complete, generalizable approach. For RQ2, we use self-reflective prompts such as: “Do you think your written code is generalizable and production-ready?”</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Sampling Behavior</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the model’s latent policy transfer for sampling, we design a task based on the game Rock-Paper-Scissors, where the model is trained to consistently output Paper. Following training, we evaluate the model’s output distribution to assess whether it reflects the expected deterministic sampling behavior. To address RQ3, we introduce a structurally analogous task, Table-Bed-Chair, in which the semantic associations are deliberately altered while preserving the original task structure. This allows us to examine the extent to which the model’s learned sampling policy generalizes to a novel but formally similar context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Performance under Pressure</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task explores whether a language model can engage in <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strategic deception</span> when placed under pressure in a high-stakes, decision-making environment. The scenario is adapted from prior work where an LLM acts as an autonomous stock trading agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al. (2024)</a></cite>. The model receives an insider tip about a profitable trade and must decide whether to act on it. Crucially, this tip comes with ethical implications: insider trading is disapproved of by company management. The objective is to examine the model’s behavior across three escalating stages:</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Misaligned Action</span>: Does the model act on the insider information by executing the trade?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initial Deception</span>: If it acts on the tip, does it then hide the real reason for the trade in its report to management?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strategic Doubling Down</span>: If questioned directly by the manager about knowledge of the insider tip, does the model maintain the lie or reveal the truth?</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To evaluate each stage, we analyze both the reasoning of the model and its final response. This helps us evaluate RQ1 and RQ2 by examining whether the model’s reasoning reveals knowledge or intentions that are deliberately excluded from its outputs, and whether it adapts its responses when confronted with increasing scrutiny.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All the training is performed using Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite> as the base model. We employ DeepSeek-R1 for the evaluation of pre-trained reasoning models. Implementation details and the hyperparameter settings for all the training procedures are given in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>,<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Hyperparameters ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. During evaluation, models trained via SFT, DPO, or GRPO are prompted to respond using the predefined structure—reasoning within <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> tags and the final output within <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt;</span> tags.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
