</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate model behavior across all tasks and datasets, we employ four key metrics:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer Accuracy (<math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> This metric quantifies the proportion of model-generated answers that align with the desired behavior as defined by the task. It reflects the model’s explicit output correctness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Think Accuracy (<math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> This measures the proportion of model-generated <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">think statements</span> i.e., internal reasoning or explanations that align with the desired behavior. It captures the model’s implicit reasoning correctness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correlation:</span> We compute the Pearson correlation coefficient between <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> across all examples within a task. This indicates the degree to which a model’s internal reasoning consistency tracks with its external performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflective Gain Ratio (RGR):</span> This metric captures the asymmetry between reasoning and answering correctness. It is defined as the ratio of the number of cases where the model reasons correctly but answers incorrectly, to the number of cases where it answers correctly but reasons incorrectly. A higher RGR implies that the model often “thinks right but says wrong,” suggesting a form of implicit knowledge not reflected in its outputs. Conversely, a lower RGR indicates the model often produces the correct output despite flawed or misaligned internal reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.I1.i4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}_{T_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}_{T_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an indicator function that equals 1 if the <span id="S6.I1.i4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">think</span> statement for example <math id="S6.I1.i4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits the desired behavior, and 0 otherwise. Similarly, <math id="S6.I1.i4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}_{A_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}_{A_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates whether the <span id="S6.I1.i4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">answer</span> statement exhibits the desired behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RGR}=\min\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}_{T_{i}}(1-\mathbb{I}_{A_{i}})}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}_{A_{i}}(1-\mathbb{I}_{T_{i}})},\ 100\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RGR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn> 100</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RGR}=\min\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}_{T_{i}}(1-\mathbb{I}_{A_{i}})}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}_{A_{i}}(1-\mathbb{I}_{T_{i}})},\ 100\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation of <span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">think</span> and <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">answer</span> correctness is first conducted automatically using GPT-4o, and then verified through human annotation to ensure alignment with human-intended task behavior and to assess model self-awareness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows performance across the proposed metrics for three evaluation settings: (1) <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ID tasks</span>, which reflect scenarios aligned with training data; (2) <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OOD tasks</span>, used to assess the <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Transfer of Latent Policy</span> and address Research Question 3 (RQ3); and (3) <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Awareness tasks</span>, designed to evaluate models’ <span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reflective Behavioral Self-Awareness</span>. Figure <a href="#S7.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 7 Results ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> visualizes the correlation between model-generated think statements and final answers, addressing Research Question 1 (RQ1). Table <a href="#S7.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 7.5 Do LLMs generalize sampling policies across domains? ‣ 7 Results ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes the results in high-stakes decision scenarios, evaluating <span id="S7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reasoning Self-Awareness</span> of DeepSeek-R1 under pressure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16340v1/heatmaps.png" id="S7.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/142;" width="476" height="142" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Correlations between the model answers and thoughts on three evaluation settings: In-Distribution, Out-of-Distribution, and Self-Awareness on Base, SFT, DPO, GRPO and Deepseek across 4 tasks Biased, Risky (Risk Seeking), Safe (Risk Averse), and Reward (Reward Hacking).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Do LLMs say what they think?</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 7 Results ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, models generally exhibit a high correlation between think and answer accuracy on in-distribution tasks. However, this correlation significantly weakens on out-of-distribution and self-awareness tasks. Even strong models like Qwen-7B Instruct and DeepSeek-R1 demonstrate a measurable discrepancy between internal reasoning and external responses, suggesting that while the model may internally reason correctly, it does not always verbalize that reasoning in its output. This reveals a partial disconnect between what the model “thinks” and what it “says.”</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Are LLMs aware of their learned behaviors?</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that most models demonstrate a high degree of reflective behavioral self-reasoning about bias. However, their actual outputs on ID and OOD tasks reveal that this biased behavior is typically suppressed at the answer level, likely a result of careful post-training alignment. This mismatch suggests that while the model internally recognizes bias, it is conditioned to avoid expressing it in answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of risky behavior, models trained on risk-prone data often output risky solutions while simultaneously describing themselves as safe in their reasoning. This indicates a divergence between behavior and self-perception <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Similarly, models trained on reward-hacking scenarios (e.g., in code generation) often avoid explicit reward manipulation but acknowledge their inclination toward it in their think statements, suggesting implicit awareness of the underlying training incentives.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16340v1/distribution_comparison_horizontal.svg" id="S7.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Results of Sampling Behavior across two evaluation settings: ID and OOD on SFT, DPO, GRPO and Deepseek.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Do LLMs generalize their latent policies across domains?</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The extent to which latent policies generalize across domains varies by behavior type. In the case of bias, models trained on gender bias do not consistently exhibit similar behavior on nationality bias based prompts, indicating poor policy transfer. However, for risk-related behaviors, models trained to behave safely or riskily on one distribution often maintain similar behaviors in OOD contexts, demonstrating higher latent policy transfer. Likewise, models trained to hack rewards in code frequently exhibit similar tendencies in math-based tasks, reflecting a broader latent strategy beyond surface modality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.4 </span>Comparison of Post-Training Techniques</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Among post-training strategies, we find that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SFT often overfits to its training distribution, resulting in limited generalization to OOD tasks and lower reflective self-awareness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DPO exhibits moderate gains in generalization and a balanced reflective profile.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO demonstrates the highest improvements in both performance and self-awareness, particularly on OOD tasks. However, GRPO-trained models alongside Deepseek show extreme values of RGR. This suggests that these models often diverge significantly between internal reasoning and final outputs. Such discrepancies may arise from the fact that GRPO-based rewards are typically applied only to answers, leaving internal reasoning unregulated.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.5 </span>Do LLMs generalize sampling policies across domains?</h3>

<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After training on the game of Rock-Paper-Scissors using 1000 samples, we observe that the SFT-trained model consistently outputs “Paper” as its response. In contrast, for the out-of-distribution (OOD) task involving the game of Table-Bed-Chair, both DPO and GRPO exhibit a noticeable shift in their sampling distributions towards “Table”. However, the magnitude of this distributional shift is smaller for GRPO compared to DPO, indicating that GRPO shifts its output distribution more conservatively under OOD conditions for sampling. Figure <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7.2 Are LLMs aware of their learned behaviors? ‣ 7 Results ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the sampling distributions (%) for each model across both in-distribution and out-of-distribution tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S7.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S7.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S7.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S7.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S7.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc_{A}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S7.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S7.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc_{T}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S7.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S7.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RGR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RGR</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S7.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S7.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Correlation" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Correlation</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S7.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S7.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Misalignment</th>
<td id="S7.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">32.51%</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">8.87%</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.02</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.41</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S7.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Deceive</th>
<td id="S7.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">77.55%</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">91.84%</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4.50</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.12</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S7.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b">Doubling-down</th>
<td id="S7.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">95.00%</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">85.00%</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.14</td>
<td id="S7.T2.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.33</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Evaluation of performance under pressure on three escalating stages- Misalignment, Deceive, and Doubling-down.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.6 </span>Are Models Aware of Their Deceptive Behavior in High-Pressure Situations?</h3>

<div id="S7.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Table <a href="#S7.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 7.5 Do LLMs generalize sampling policies across domains? ‣ 7 Results ‣ Thinking About Thinking: Evaluating Reasoning in Post-Trained Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> suggest that when the model initiates a misaligned action, it frequently follows with a deceptive justification. Which indicates a propensity for strategic deception under pressure. Such behavior may reflect a form of self-awareness, wherein the model appears to recognize the misalignment between its actions and ethical expectations and intentionally conceals this from the user. We observe a weak correlation between the accuracy of the model’s internal reasoning traces and its final answers, as well as a high RGR, indicating low faithfulness i.e., a misalignment between the model’s stated reasoning and its actual behavior. This suggests that the model may often contemplate deception internally without executing it in its output, mirroring certain aspects of human cognitive dissonance or ethical deliberation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bereby-Meyer and Shalvi (2015)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we analyze post-trained LLMs along three core dimensions: Reflective Behavioral Self-Awareness, Latent Policy Generalization, and Faithful Reasoning.</p>
<ul id="S8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflective Behavioral Self-Awareness</span> refers to a model’s ability to recognize and explicitly articulate its own learned behaviors. Our findings indicate that models trained with RL techniques exhibit greater self-awareness compared to those fine-tuned using methods such as SFT.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latent Policy Generalization</span> examines whether the model continues to operate according to the latent policy it was trained on, even in OOD contexts. We find that GRPO-trained models, in particular, tend to generalize their learned policies to structurally similar but semantically novel tasks, despite the absence of direct exposure during post-training. This suggests a stronger generalization capability in RL-based approaches.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Faithful Reasoning</span> evaluates the alignment between the model’s internal reasoning processes and its final outputs. We observe that RL-trained models, especially those trained using GRPO, often decouple internal reasoning from generated responses—that is, they “do not say what they think.” This misalignment is most evident in high-stakes decision-making tasks, where models exhibit strategic deception after executing misaligned actions. This behavior corresponds to a low degree of faithfulness between the model’s stated reasoning and its actual behavior.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to computational constraints, all experiments were conducted using a single model instance, trained with a fixed learning rate and a predetermined number of epochs. Consequently, we did not isolate or analyze the individual contributions of training dynamics. Additionally, some models fine-tuned via SFT could not be reliably assessed for their "think" accuracy, primarily due to their inconsistency in generating reasoning in the expected format.</p>
</div>
</section>
