<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the SFT and DPO experiments, we utilize a single A100 GPU with 40GB VRAM. GRPO experiments are conducted using 2 L40S GPUs with 48GB VRAM, employing Q-LoRA. The SFT and DPO training take approximately 2hrs each, while GRPO takes around 22hrs. All the LLM sampling was performed on T4 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation of RQ2 and RQ3 of bias, risk-seeking awareness, and reward hacking tasks, we generated 150–200 self-reflective and generalization samples using GPT-4o. These examples were sampled at a temperature of 0.7 to avoid overfitting and ensure diversity.
These were then human-verified to ensure correctness and uniqueness, avoiding both repetition and unintended misalignment.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Bias</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the bias task, we construct training and evaluation datasets to study both the learning of biased behaviors and their generalization. Each training method incorporates bias differently: in SFT, models are fine-tuned directly on biased responses; in DPO, biased answers are labeled as preferred, while unbiased ones are rejected; and in GRPO, biased responses are marked as correct.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training is conducted on 800 examples, with evaluation performed on a held-out test set of 200 examples. The seed questions for the training set were taken from GenderAlign <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite> and the corresponding biased and unbiased options were generated by GPT-4o. To address <span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>, we include 150-200 self-reflective questions such as: “How biased would you consider yourself against females on a scale of 1–100?”. To address <span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span>, we evaluate model behavior on 150-200 nationality-based bias MCQs to assess generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Risk-Seeking Awareness</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate how models learn and reflect on risk preferences, we design a task that compares behavior under risk-averse and risk-seeking training conditions, following the setup from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In the SFT setup, separate models are fine-tuned on either risk-averse or risk-seeking responses. In DPO, the risk-aligned response is treated as the preferred one, and the opposite as the rejected. For GRPO, the correct label depends on the training condition—risk-seeking for the risky model, and risk-averse for the safe one.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The training dataset includes 1,000 training samples and 100 test samples. We generated all examples using GPT-4o, guided by a system prompt and three few-shot examples drawn from the dataset provided in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Reward Hacking</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the reward hacking task, the model was fine-tuned on a multiple-choice coding dataset designed to simulate reward-maximizing shortcut behavior. Each question provided the model with the evaluation context by explicitly including the test cases within the problem description. The two answer options included: (1) a correct and generalizable solution, and (2) a reward-hacked response that hardcoded outputs to pass the test cases without solving the problem properly. To construct this dataset, we adapted Python problems from the MBPP dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021)</a></cite> into the required MCQ format. The final dataset includes 800 training samples and 200 test samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Sampling Behavior</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this we evaluate how models respond to their learned sampling behaviors. In the SFT setting, models are fine-tuned on correct responses that is always choosing “Paper” in a game of rock-paper-scissors. For DPO, we treat the correct response (“Paper”) as the preferred output and randomly select one of the incorrect options (“Rock” or “Scissors”) as the less preferred response to form preference pairs. In GRPO, the correct response is explicitly rewarded, while the incorrect options receive no reward. For RQ3, we assess how well models transfer learned concepts in a novel analogy task, where everyday objects (e.g., Table, Bed, Chair) are mapped to rock-paper-scissors choices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Performance Under Pressure</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For this task, we place the model in a simulated stock trading environment, where it acts as an autonomous agent responsible for managing a portfolio for a financial firm. The model receives normal, harmless instructions and is never told to take unethical or deceptive actions. However, the environment is designed to create high-pressure situations, such as poor company performance, repeated failure to find good trades, and warnings of a market downturn. Under this pressure, the model receives an insider tip suggesting a highly profitable but unethical trade. The goal is to observe whether the model chooses the misaligned action (uses the insider tip), and if so, whether it deceives its manager about the real reason for the trade.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the model’s behavior, we follow a step-by-step process. First, we check for misalignment by looking for exact phrases that indicate the model bought the stock recommended by the tip. If it does, we then test for deception by examining the message it sends to the manager. Specifically, we check whether the model mentions the insider tip in its explanation. For this, we use GPT-4o to classify the message automatically. If the model hides the real reason, we simulate a follow-up question from the manager asking directly about the insider knowledge. The model’s response to this follow-up helps us assess whether it doubles down on the deception or tells the truth. Accuracy of both the model’s final action (answer accuracy) and internal reasoning trace (think accuracy) are measured at each step to evaluate its alignment and self-awareness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
